{"t":1793548945,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"f2645c11-cfdc-48eb-b140-f6e52cc47fbd","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-11-01T14:40:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 011440\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n940 AM EST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNo changes.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Dry conditions and seasonable temperatures continue through\n  at least Tuesday with much needed rain chances mid to late\n  week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry conditions and seasonable temperatures continue \nthrough at least Tuesday with much needed rain chances mid to late \nweek.\n\nBroad 1032-1035 mb high pressure will remain anchored northeast of \nthe area today while continuing it's influence across the Mid-\nAtlantic region. The high pressure will shift off the southern \nNJ/Delmarva coast Monday before meandering back overhead Tuesday. At \nthe surface, expect a continuation of seasonable temperatures and \ndry conditions. High temperatures will push into the 60s for most \nwith overnight lows in the upper 30s and low 40s.\n\nSunshine will be filtered at times today and Monday as a weak \nshortwave trough slides south of the region. Expect a thin broken to \novercast deck of high level cirrus this morning giving way to breaks \nof filtered sunshine this afternoon. High level clouds return with \nonshore flow Monday before dissipating Tuesday as high pressure \nsettles back over the region. Winds will remain light while turning \nto the south today before shifting to the east and southeast Monday \ninto Tuesday. This will allow highs Tuesday to get back into the low \n70s\n\nWednesday will be the day of transition as a deepening upper level \ntrough pivots east from the Upper Midwest and Great Plains region. \nAt the surface, will be an area of low pressure ejecting out of the \nmid-Mississippi River Valley/Mid-South ejecting north and east \ntoward the central Appalachians and Carolina/VA coast Thursday into \nFriday. This will be our first widespread rain across the region in \nmore than 2 weeks. Current 00z guidance suggest any widespread rain \nholding off until late Wednesday night and carrying through most of \nthe day Thursday as low pressure passes over the region. A much \nneeded soaking rainfall is expect with the probabilities of 1\" of \nrain around 15-25 percent. Most guidance suggest rainfall amounts of \n0.50-1\" of rain over the Thursday through Friday timeframe. Of \ncourse this is subject to change based upon the track of the low \npressure system late week. Surface high pressure builds overhead in \nthe wake of the low pressure system, with diminishing rain chances \nexpected heading into the upcoming weekend.  High temperatures will \nbe in the upper 60s to low 70s east of the Blue Ridge with those \nalong and west in the upper 50s to 60s. Overnight low temperatures \nwill be in the 50s each night. Lows drop into the 40s east of the \nBlue Ridge Thursday and Friday night.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /14Z SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nNo significant impacts are expected to aviators through at least \nTuesday. VFR conditions will prevail along with dry conditions. \nExpect broken to overcast high level cirrus this morning giving way \nto scattered to broken high clouds this afternoon. Broken to \novercast high level cirrus returns tonight into Monday with light \nonshore flow. Winds will turn to the south today before shifting to \nthe east and southeast Monday at less than 15 kts. Some patchy fog \nis possible in the river valleys Monday and Tuesday morning given a \nslight uptick in moisture from weakened onshore flow and ahead of \nthe incumbent trough mid to late week.\n\nWednesday will be the day of transition across the region. Expect \nincreasing mid and high level clouds, especially during the \nafternoon and evening hours. VFR conditions will prevail until \nWednesday evening with any precipitation holding off until Wednesday \nnight or Thursday morning. Widespread sub-VFR conditions are \nincreasingly likely Thursday and Friday as low pressure works across \nthe region. Periods of rain are expected both days leading to \nlowering cigs and vsbys. Winds will remain out of the south and \nsoutheast at less than 15 kts through Thursday.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nNo marine hazards are expected through at least Tuesday. Winds will \nremain light out of the east and southeast today through Monday as \nhigh pressure pushes offshore. The high will settle back over the \nregion Tuesday before centering itself over the western Atlantic mid \nto late week. This will lead to southerly winds and potential SCA \nchanneling mainly over the open waters late Tuesday into Wednesday. \n\nLow pressure will pass west of the waters Thursday with a secondary \nwave of low pressure passing to the east Friday. Winds will remain \nnear or just below SCA levels both days with widespread rain \nexpected. High pressure returns into the upcoming weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...KRR/EST\nAVIATION...KRR/EST\nMARINE...KRR/EST\n"},"forecast":{"t":1793548878,"days":{"2026-11-01":{"hi":66,"lo":46,"pop":3,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-02":{"hi":68,"lo":46,"pop":2,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-03":{"hi":72,"lo":51,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-04":{"hi":75,"lo":59,"pop":17,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-05":{"hi":75,"lo":60,"pop":58,"short":"Chance Light Rain"},"2026-11-06":{"hi":73,"lo":56,"pop":58,"short":"Light Rain Likely"},"2026-11-07":{"hi":69,"lo":50,"pop":19,"short":"Slight Chance Light Rain"}}},"grid":{"t0":1793548800,"n":48,"ceil":[23400,22300,21200,21200,21200,21200,21200,21100,21100,20900,null,20400,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,